course: dictionary
lesson: 9276
title: Spaghetti and its other meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Spaghetti is a noun for a kind of pasta made in long, thin strings. You boil it and eat it with a sauce. Context helps you spot which meaning is being used: food words sit near it, like «spaghetti bolognese» or «spaghetti with meatballs».
explain: Spaghetti can also be something you build with. «First activity: build a tower out of dry spaghetti and marshmallows» uses dry spaghetti in a lesson, not a meal. The clue is the word build, not food.
explain: Sometimes spaghetti is a picture word. «Spaghetti junction» means a road junction that is very tangled, with roads twisting over and under each other. Ask what would fit: a messy, twisty road, not a dinner. Then try your meaning in the sentence to check it.
fact: Spaghetti is a kind of pasta made in long, thin strings.
fact: Spaghetti is boiled and eaten with a sauce, such as «spaghetti bolognese».
fact: Spaghetti comes from Italy, and it is also used in science and maths to build shapes and test strength.
fact: In 1957 the BBC showed a spoof film of spaghetti «growing on trees», and many viewers believed it.
exercise: What is spaghetti? | A tall, thin bridge made of wood | A kind of tree that grows in Italy | *Pasta-shaped sweets that you eat with a spoon | Pasta in long, thin strings, boiled and eaten with a sauce
exercise: «First activity: build a tower out of dry spaghetti and marshmallows.» What is the spaghetti used for in this sentence? | *Building a tower in a lesson | Cooking a meal | Growing on trees
exercise: Someone says, «That junction is a real spaghetti junction!» What does it mean? | A quiet country lane | *A very tangled road junction | A wide road with only one lane | A junction with a pasta shop
test: In 1957 the BBC showed a spoof of spaghetti «growing on trees». What is a spoof? | A type of pasta | A kind of cooking | A true report about farming | *A joke or trick that is not true
test: Why do people call a messy road junction a spaghetti junction? | Because it is a food shop | Because it is in Italy | *Because the roads are tangled like spaghetti | Because the road is made of pasta
exam: Which sentence uses spaghetti to mean pasta? | «First activity: build a tower out of dry spaghetti.» | «The road was a messy spaghetti junction.» | *«We boiled the spaghetti and ate it with a sauce.»
